From 5626225641b05b36868417dfbc40fd715cd987b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 05:23:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/23] dos: Count what BEGIN_DIR streams over
 DoSBeginDirMaxStreams ask for

Before anyone enables DoSBeginDirLimitEnabled, operators need to know
what it would refuse. begindir_over_max counts the streams over the
limit, but not what they carry: a flood's empty or onion service
streams, or clients' ordinary directory fetches. DoSBeginDirMaxStreams
is the one limit that applies to every kind of directory request, so
that is its collateral damage.

When dos_should_refuse_begindir() finds a BEGIN_DIR over the limit, it
now also marks the stream, and connection_exit_connect_dir() copies the
mark to the directory connection. Count each such request once, by
type, with the other request counters, as
tor_relay_begindir_request_over_max_total{type}. Under enforcement
these streams are refused, so the family counts only while the limit is
off; refusals then show as BEGIN_DIR cells less
tor_relay_streams_total{type="BEGIN_DIR"}.

Part of #41339.
